I'm a strong advocate for mandatory affordable housing set-asides because they directly address one of the most fundamental problems of our time—the racial and economic segregation that's been built into American cities for generations. When developers are required to include affordable units in new developments, we create mixed-income communities where working people and low-income families can live safely and access quality education and jobs. This is about collective welfare and ensuring everyone has a stake in building a strong, equitable society.

While I understand the argument that these requirements reduce supply, the real issue is that we've allowed market forces to operate without sufficient constraints on wealth concentration. We have enormous wealth inequality, and requiring developers to include affordable units is a reasonable policy tool to correct that imbalance
